## Why This Is Asked

To assess how candidates craft end-to-end performance tests for ultra-low-latency pipelines with multiple services and external dependencies, ensuring backpressure, fail-fast behavior, and autoscaling.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end and tail latency
- Bounded buffers, backpressure
- Circuit breakers and fail-fast
- Traffic shaping and ramp-up
- Observability and tracing (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger)
- Kubernetes autoscaling (HPA, KEDA optional)

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { vus: 1000, duration: '60s' };
export default function () {
  const r = http.post('https://ads.example.com/bid', JSON.stringify({bid: 1}), { headers: { 'Content-Type':'application/json' }});
  check(r, { 'status 200': (r)=> r.status===200 });
  sleep(0.0005);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure and isolate downstream latency vs upstream latency in a live campaign?
- How would you simulate upstream outages without affecting other tenants in a shared cluster?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design an end-to-end test with real-world dependencies (MongoDB, external service) and basic backpressure, plus simple workload modeling. It checks how a candidate isolates bottlenecks across validation, DB writes, and external calls.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency measurement
- Workload modeling and pacing
- Bottleneck identification across components
- Basic backpressure strategies and error-rate monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
